Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: do not request a role switch for peripheral-class devices

When accepting an incoming ACL connection, the host asks to become central
as soon as one listening socket has role switch set (bluetoothd's SDP server
and A2DP do). After that role switch, DualShock 3 clones (ShanWan, Gasia)
stop answering the host's L2CAP signalling (Information and Configure
requests), so the HID channels are never configured and the pad drops.

Stay peripheral when the Class of Device major class is Peripheral (0x05:
keyboards, mice, gamepads), which have no use for the role switch.

Heuristic: an upstreamable version would rather use a per-device flag set
by bluetoothd. Proven by A/B test (same L2CAP server, with and without a
socket requesting central).
---
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -3354,7 +3354,13 @@
 
 		bacpy(&cp.bdaddr, &ev->bdaddr);
 
-		if (lmp_rswitch_capable(hdev) && (mask & HCI_LM_MASTER))
+		/* Some HID peripherals (e.g. Sixaxis clones) stop answering
+		 * L2CAP signalling after a role switch at accept time, and
+		 * a HID peripheral has no use for it: stay peripheral for
+		 * devices whose Class of Device major class is Peripheral.
+		 */
+		if (lmp_rswitch_capable(hdev) && (mask & HCI_LM_MASTER) &&
+		    (ev->dev_class[1] & 0x1f) != 0x05)
 			cp.role = 0x00; /* Become central */
 		else
 			cp.role = 0x01; /* Remain peripheral */
